feat(bench): typical frontend and backend application fixtures

What:
Five fixtures covering the application shapes customers actually deploy: a Vite
React SPA, a Next.js SSR app, an Express TypeScript API, a FastAPI service, and
a PHP-FPM stack with composer and a Vite asset build.

Why:
The existing targets measure mechanics - fan-out, overhead floor, artifact size,
secrets. None of them looks like a customer's application, and dependency
install is what dominates a real build. Without these the benchmark would
report how Qovery handles contrived work rather than ordinary work.

Notes:
- Versions are pinned exactly, not ranged, so a rerun resolves the same tree.
- Manifests are copied before sources in every Dockerfile, so a source change
  does not reinstall dependencies. The PHP one carries a comment saying so,
  because getting that ordering wrong is the most common cause of slow
  customer rebuilds we have seen.
- The PHP stack mirrors a real customer Dockerfile: composer plus pnpm-style
  asset build, PHP-FPM behind nginx.
